// Rewriting a file from one spelling of the language to the next.
//
// The tool exists so that a rename is a MIGRATION and not a break: a family
// of keywords changes, the old spelling keeps parsing for one release, and
// this is what carries the repo — and everybody else's files — across in
// between. It lands before the first rename rather than after it, because a
// migration written after the fact is one nobody's files ever went through.
//
// Pure and target-agnostic like `viewfile` and `statedef`: source text in,
// rewritten source and the edits that produced it out, never touching the
// filesystem. The CLI does the I/O.
//
// It never re-serialises an AST. Every edit is a (span, replacement) pair
// taken from a lexer's own spans and applied to the ORIGINAL text, so a file
// that goes through comes back with its comments, its blank lines and its
// alignment exactly where the author left them. `Decl::to_source` would
// normalise all three away, which is why it is not used here.
import {
  "marianoguerra/tutuca/core" @tutuca,
  "marianoguerra/tutuca/viewfile",
  "marianoguerra/tutuca/tscript",
  "moonbitlang/core/debug",
}

// The golden corpus is every view file and card in the repo, read from disk —
// so the test needs the filesystem and the CLI's own path walk, which is why
// it lives beside the CLI rather than here. What is here is the contract.
import {
  "moonbitlang/core/debug",
} for "test"
